      "title": "Art & Design Education in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the scope, institutional history, and documented challenges of art and design education in Iran."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A broad family of disciplines",
        "content": "Art and design is a useful umbrella, but it should not suggest a single course of study. A research article by Nahid Amiri and Nahid Abdi groups Iranian higher art education into architecture, urban planning, applied arts, visual arts, performing arts, and music. These fields share an interest in making and interpretation while addressing different objects, audiences, and professional settings. [s1]\n\nThe University of Tehran offers a concrete institutional example. Its College of Fine Arts directory links to architecture, urban planning, visual arts, industrial design, music, and performing arts. That arrangement shows why a guide to Iranian art education must look beyond painting alone. It also illustrates the proximity of design and the built environment to disciplines concerned with images, sound, and performance. The directory establishes organizational coverage; it does not establish the current curriculum or admission requirements of every program. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "International models and local institutions",
        "content": "Formal architectural education offers one route into the history of this academic landscape. Peyman Akhgar's study of the Beaux-Arts system examines the influence of the Paris-based École des Beaux-Arts on architectural education in twentieth-century Iran. Its accessible abstract identifies graduates of that school as important participants in establishing the University of Tehran's Faculty of Fine Arts. [s3]\n\nThe significance is educational as well as stylistic: institutions and teaching models travelled alongside architects. This provides a more precise starting point than treating modern Iranian art education as either wholly imported or entirely separate from international developments. However, the abstract alone cannot establish how particular studio exercises, assessment practices, or departmental curricula changed over time. Those details require inspection of the full study and institutional records. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evaluation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Access, teaching, and professional preparation",
        "content": "Amiri and Abdi's study examines higher art education over the period it labels 2011–2020, using statistics supplied by Iran's Institute for Research and Planning in Higher Education. Its abstract reports concerns about the uneven geographical distribution of institutions and disciplines, student selection, professional skills, and quality assurance. These are the authors' research findings for a defined study period, rather than a verified description of every institution today. [s1]\n\nFor readers comparing programs, these findings suggest several useful questions: where is a discipline available, what facilities support it, how does assessment recognize practical work, and how does study connect with professional practice? These are editorial questions prompted by the research, not findings about any particular university. The evidence inspected here does not support a national ranking, a current employment rate for art graduates, or a claim that facilities and opportunities are uniform across provinces. A fuller account needs departmental curricula and comparable regional evidence before drawing those conclusions. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
