    "description": "Research and technology in Iran includes fundamental inquiry, specialized research institutions, and organizations intended to connect scientific work with commercial applications. Historical evidence documents expanding innovation support alongside uneven links between research, investment, and markets.",
    "tags": ["Research", "Technology", "Innovation", "Science policy", "Patents", "Research institutions"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T17:30:20Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T17:30:20Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Research & Technology in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran's research and innovation landscape through documented institutions, historical patent data, and evidence limits."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "research-and-application",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From scientific questions to practical applications",
        "content": "Research and technology encompasses activities with different purposes and timescales. An institution working on mathematical questions should not be judged solely by immediate product sales, while a technology incubator needs evidence about the businesses it supports. The sources inspected for this overview illustrate both ends of that spectrum: international mathematical training involving the Institute for Research in Fundamental Sciences, and innovation support described in UNESCO's science reporting. [s1][s4]\n\nThese examples should be read as parts of a landscape rather than as a single institutional chain. Co-organizing a scientific workshop does not imply responsibility for national innovation policy. Equally, hosting entrepreneurs does not demonstrate that research has become a commercially successful product. Keeping these distinctions visible helps readers interpret institutional announcements and performance statistics. [s1][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "innovation-support",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The growth of innovation support",
        "content": "UNESCO's 2021 Iran overview describes an expansion of incubators, accelerators, and innovation centres. It explains that campus incubators provided graduate entrepreneurs with shared working space and mentoring. In that account, universities were therefore involved not only in education and research but also in supporting attempts to establish businesses. These are documented support mechanisms, not proof that every supported enterprise survived or generated additional innovation. [s1]\n\nThe same overview describes tensions between policy ambition and economic conditions. It associates a decline in knowledge-based exports with the reimposition of United States sanctions, while also reporting incentives for firms to seek domestic suppliers. It notes weak business investment in research despite policy support. These observations belong to the report's historical coverage and cannot establish conditions in October 2026. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "research-for-public-needs",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Research for public needs",
        "content": "Commercialization is only one reason to maintain research capacity. Earthquake risk provides a documented example of research connected to public safety and regional cooperation. UNESCO records an agreement signed on 16 February 2021 establishing a regional education and research centre on earthquake risk management and resilience at Iran's International Institute of Earthquake Engineering and Seismology. Its stated geographical remit covered West and Central Asia. [s3]\n\nUNESCO describes its associated Category 2 centres as contributing through research, advanced training, information exchange, and capacity building. This establishes the intended institutional role. It does not, by itself, demonstrate reduced disaster losses, improved building safety, or the effectiveness of a particular training program. Those outcomes require separate evaluation. The example nevertheless broadens the meaning of technology policy beyond patents and start-ups to include the production and exchange of knowledge for public purposes. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "patent-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What patent counts show",
        "content": "UNESCO's historical figure reports patent grants at Iran's Intellectual Property Centre. The accompanying dataset retains the missing reporting year rather than estimating it. The pattern is uneven: the reported grant count declines before recovering in the final observations, which remain below the first observation. [s2]\n\nThese are administrative grant counts, not a count of technologies successfully commercialized. They also should not be equated with the separate international patent-office measure shown elsewhere in the same figure. The underlying national monitoring report was not inspected, so detailed counting rules and calendar treatment remain unresolved. For that reason, the values are presented in a table using the source's year labels, without a trend chart that could suggest stronger comparability than has been verified. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "patent-observations",
        "type": "table",
        "content": {
          "title": "Patent grants reported at Iran's Intellectual Property Centre, 2012–2017",
          "dataset_id": "national-patent-grants"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading institutions and evidence together",
        "content": "Scientific exchange offers another perspective on research activity. An ICTP–IPM workshop announcement describes advanced mathematical teaching followed by a conference intended for presenting and discussing research. Such records help establish specific collaborations, but they cannot substitute for national evidence on staffing, research expenditure, or long-term output. [s4]\n\nA balanced assessment therefore needs several kinds of evidence: institutional documentation for responsibilities, research records for scientific activity, and consistently defined statistics for change over time. The material inspected here supports a historical introduction. A current account still requires updated expenditure and researcher series, fuller coverage of specialized institutes, and independent evaluation of innovation programs.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
